<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    ExperianIDVerifySDK privacy manifest (Apple "App Privacy Configuration").

    Placement contract
    ==================
    Source (SPM):  Sources/ExperianIDVerifySDK/PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy, declared as a
                   target resource in Package.swift. SwiftPM does NOT auto-detect
                   privacy manifests — the explicit `resources:` entry is required.
    XCFramework:   one copy at the root of every iOS `.framework` slice. Enforced by
                   Scripts/validate-vendor-xcframework.sh and
                   Scripts/validate-internal-xcframework.sh.

    Editing rules
    =============
    * Only the enumerated NSPrivacyCollectedDataType / ...Purpose / ...APIType /
      ...APITypeReasons constants Apple publishes are legal values. App Store Connect
      rejects unknown keys or values (ITMS-91056) and undeclared required-reason API
      use (ITMS-91053).
    * `NSPrivacyTracking` is false and `NSPrivacyTrackingDomains` is empty. Those two
      MUST stay consistent: `false` with a non-empty domain list is an invalid
      manifest. Flipping tracking on is a product/legal decision, not a code change.
    * Fraud and verification collection is scoped to `...PurposeAppFunctionality`,
      whose Apple definition covers "prevent fraud" and "implement security measures".
      Native SDK timing, lifecycle breadcrumbs, and their opaque tracking identifier
      are additionally declared under `...PurposeAnalytics`: the flow works identically
      whether or not those records are delivered. Nothing here is collected for
      advertising or personalization.

    !! Collection happens in the WebView, not in Swift — do not "clean up" !!
    =======================================================================
    Most of what this manifest declares is collected by the Embedded Experience
    web component the SDK loads into its WKWebView, NOT by code under Sources/.
    NeuroID (behavioral analytics) and FraudNet/Experian Device Intelligence
    (device fingerprinting) run as JavaScript INSIDE that component. Grepping the
    native sources for them finds nothing, and the `FraudSignals/` protocol seam
    (`BehavioralAnalyticsManaging`, `DeviceFingerprintProviding`) is currently
    unwired — none of that makes these declarations removable.

    Section 6 is native: Swift measures bootstrap timing and records a bounded
    lifecycle breadcrumb stream through the SDK's own analytics pipeline.

    Apple holds an SDK responsible for data collected through it, including data
    collected by web content it presents. The SDK owns the WKWebView, chooses the
    document, and is the reason the collectors run at all, so their attributes
    belong in THIS manifest. Deleting them because "the SDK doesn't do that"
    would under-declare collection that ships today — the failure mode Apple
    actually rejects, and the one that misrepresents every host app's privacy
    nutrition label.

    The corollary: this manifest must be re-reviewed whenever the embedded
    component's data collection changes, even though no Swift file changed.
    EmbeddedExperienceKind / EmbeddedComponentSpec selects that component.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
        Tracking, as defined by App Tracking Transparency, means joining this data
        with third-party data for advertising, ad measurement, or data-broker
        sharing. This SDK does none of that: identity and fraud signals are used
        solely for identity verification and fraud detection/prevention, which
        Apple's User Privacy and Data Use FAQ exempts from the ATT permission
        requirement. Consequently every collected type below also carries
        NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking = false.
    -->
    <key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
    <false/>
    <key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
    <array/>

    <!--
        Required-reason APIs: none.

        The SDK does not touch UserDefaults, file timestamps, disk space, active
        keyboards, ProcessInfo.systemUptime, or mach_absolute_time(). Elapsed-time
        measurement for the bootstrap telemetry in section 6 uses ContinuousClock,
        which is not one of Apple's five required-reason categories (the covered
        clock APIs are systemUptime and mach_absolute_time). WebKit
        (WKWebView / WKWebsiteDataStore) is not a required-reason category either.

        The in-WebView collectors do not change this. Required-reason API rules
        cover calls into the listed Objective-C/Swift platform APIs; JavaScript
        running in a WKWebView reaches none of them — it sees only what WebKit
        exposes to web content. So the collection declared below is real, while
        this array is legitimately empty.

        If a future change introduces one of the five covered API categories, add
        the entry here — a third-party SDK may not rely on the host app's manifest
        to declare its own required-reason API use.
    -->
    <key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
    <array/>

    <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
    <array>
        <!--
            ============================================================
            1. Identity verification flow (the SDK's primary function)
            ============================================================
            Collected from the applicant through the Embedded Experience
            <identity-verification> component the SDK presents, and transmitted to
            Experian to perform the verification decision. All of it is linked to
            the identity being verified by definition.

            Provenance: this section is justified by the SDK's product function,
            NOT by the NeuroID/FraudNet data-attributes summary that sourced
            sections 2-5. That document scopes itself to the two fraud-signal
            vendors and never mentions applicant PII. An auditor diffing this
            manifest against that document alone will find no backing for the five
            types below — that is expected, and is not grounds for deleting them.
        -->

        <!-- Legal name supplied to the verification component. -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeName</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
            </array>
        </dict>

        <!-- Residential/mailing address used to corroborate the identity. -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePhysicalAddress</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
            </array>
        </dict>

        <!-- Email address used as a verification and contact signal. -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeEmailAddress</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
            </array>
        </dict>

        <!-- Phone number used as a verification and contact signal. -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePhoneNumber</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
            </array>
        </dict>

        <!--
            Government-issued identifiers (for example SSN or national ID) and
            date of birth collected by the identity-verification component.

            Not Sensitive Info: Apple defines that bucket narrowly as racial or
            ethnic data, sexual orientation, pregnancy or childbirth information,
            disability, religious or philosophical beliefs, trade union
            membership, political opinion, genetic information, or biometric
            data. Government identifiers and date of birth are none of those, so
            they are declared under Other Data Types instead.

            Declared once, at the bottom of this array, together with the
            device/environment fingerprinting attributes from section 5: both
            provenances resolve to the same Apple data type with the same
            linkage, tracking flag, and purpose, and Apple's schema admits one
            entry per data type. Two identical dicts would be a redundant
            declaration, not a more precise one.
        -->

        <!--
            No Photos or Videos entry, deliberately. The MVP performs no document
            media capture and handles no photos: there is no camera capture, no
            document/selfie capture, and no image upload in scope. Nothing native
            supports it either — the SDK sets webView.uiDelegate = nil, installs
            no open-panel or media-capture handler, and never requests camera
            access.

            Do not add NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePhotosorVideos speculatively.
            Declaring collection that does not happen misstates the App Privacy
            nutrition label of every host app. If document capture or upload
            enters scope later, the embedded component gains it without any Swift
            change here, so that scope decision must come with a manifest update.
        -->

        <!--
            ============================================================
            2. Fraud-signal identifiers (collected in-WebView)
            ============================================================
            Covers the NeuroID "session linkage" attribute (an anonymous session
            identifier generated by NeuroID or supplied by client-side code) and
            the FraudNet "account linkage supplied by the client" attribute, plus
            the SDK's own correlation/tracking identifiers carried on bridge
            events. Used to associate events belonging to one verification
            session; never joined with third-party advertising data.
        -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
            </array>
        </dict>

        <!--
            FraudNet "device and profile identifiers": device print / device ID
            and device profile ID. Used to recognise a returning device for
            fraud scoring only — not for advertising, attribution, or measurement.

            Deliberately no advertising-identifier (IDFA) claim here. The source
            document hedges this attribute ("where available"; "availability
            depends on platform and configuration"), and on iOS the IDFA is only
            reachable via ATTrackingManager with explicit user permission —
            JavaScript in a WKWebView cannot read it, and this SDK never requests
            ATT authorization (NSPrivacyTracking is false, above). Asserting IDFA
            collection while NSPrivacyTracking = false would be exactly the
            contradiction App Review flags, so the claim is struck unless FraudNet
            confirms it applies to this iOS WKWebView integration.
        -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeDeviceID</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
            </array>
        </dict>

        <!--
            ============================================================
            3. Behavioural interaction signals (NeuroID, in-WebView)
            ============================================================
            Keystroke timing and rhythm, taps, swipes, scrolling, and other
            navigation or interaction events, observed by NeuroID's JavaScript
            within the embedded component. The native SDK also records a bounded
            lifecycle vocabulary (launch, component lifecycle, completion, and
            teardown) for SDK-health analytics. Neither path captures characters
            typed, so no User Content type is declared for it.
        -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeProductInteraction</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
            </array>
        </dict>

        <!--
            ============================================================
            4. Network-derived location (NeuroID + FraudNet, in-WebView)
            ============================================================
            NeuroID "network context": hashed or de-identified IP address,
            IP-derived geolocation where available and permitted, VPN indicators.
            FraudNet "network and location": IP address or network block, ISP name,
            IP-derived country/city, proxy or VPN indicators, carrier and network
            characteristics.

            This is coarse, IP-derived location only. The SDK requests no
            CoreLocation authorisation and collects no GPS fix, so
            NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePreciseLocation is deliberately absent.
        -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeCoarseLocation</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
            </array>
        </dict>

        <!--
            ============================================================
            5. Device, environment, and integrity signals (in-WebView)
            ============================================================
            Apple publishes no dedicated data type for device-fingerprinting
            attributes, so the remaining NeuroID and FraudNet categories are
            declared under Other Data Types:

              NeuroID  - browser context (user-agent, browser type/version,
                         private-browsing status, plugin information) and device
                         context (operating system, device/browser environment,
                         automation-framework indicators).
              FraudNet - browser and HTTP metadata (user-agent, accepted language,
                         character set, content types and encoding, navigator
                         platform and CPU class, referrer, payload status);
                         screen and display (width, height, available dimensions,
                         resolution); operating system and platform (name, version,
                         platform and device type); mobile and native-app signals
                         (device name and ID, carrier name and country code,
                         Android ID, hashed SIM serial, native-app risk
                         indicators); time and locale (device timestamp, time-zone
                         offset, DST status, language settings); device integrity
                         and environment (emulator, virtual machine, jailbreak or
                         root, spoofing, bot or automation, malformed payload, and
                         missing or inconsistent identifier indicators); and
                         familiarity and activity history (whether a device print,
                         IP, ISP, time zone, browser, OS, carrier, or SIM is
                         familiar for an account, plus event counts and device
                         reuse velocity over defined time windows).

            Availability of individual attributes varies by platform, OS
            permissions, client configuration, and applicable consent
            requirements; this entry declares the superset. The source document
            lists web- and Android-specific attributes (Android ID, SIM serial,
            plugin lists) that an iOS WKWebView will not surface — they are
            covered by the same superset rather than pruned, since the component
            decides what it collects and can change without an SDK release.

            This single entry also carries the government-identifier and
            date-of-birth declaration from section 1 (see the note there).
        -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherDataTypes</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
            </array>
        </dict>

        <!--
            ============================================================
            6. Native SDK analytics
            ============================================================
            Unlike everything above, this one IS collected by Swift code in this
            package: WebViewLoadTimingLogger measures bootstrap durations, while
            FlowAnalyticsSession records the presentation's lifecycle breadcrumbs.
            Both post to Experian's Embedded Experiences analytics ingest on the
            same MFE lane the component itself loads from.

            Declared as Analytics rather than App Functionality, honestly: the
            flow works exactly the same whether or not these events are
            delivered. They measure SDK health, handoff, completion, and where the
            bootstrap spends its time.

            Linked, because records carry the component's opaque trackingId plus a
            random per-presentation sdk_flow_id. No token, bridge detail string,
            verification sessionId, URL, status, or other experienceDetails field
            crosses into native analytics.

            Tracking is false and stays false. These events are first-party
            performance measurements sent to Experian; they are never joined with
            third-party data, used for advertising or ad measurement, or shared
            with data brokers, which is what App Tracking Transparency means by
            tracking.
        -->
        <dict>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
            <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePerformanceData</string>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
            <true/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
            <false/>
            <key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
            <array>
                <string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
